       The  ppiiddffdd__sseenndd__ssiiggnnaall() system call sends the signal _s_i_g to the target
       process referred to by _p_i_d_f_d, a PID file descriptor that  refers  to  a
       process.

       If  the  _i_n_f_o argument points to a _s_i_g_i_n_f_o___t buffer, that buffer should
       be populated as described in rrtt__ssiiggqquueeuueeiinnffoo(2).

       If the _i_n_f_o argument is a null pointer, this is equivalent to  specify‐
       ing  a pointer to a _s_i_g_i_n_f_o___t buffer whose fields match the values that
       are implicitly supplied when a signal is sent using kkiillll(2):

       •  _s_i___s_i_g_n_o is set to the signal number;
       •  _s_i___e_r_r_n_o is set to 0;
       •  _s_i___c_o_d_e is set to SSII__UUSSEERR;
       •  _s_i___p_i_d is set to the caller’s PID; and
       •  _s_i___u_i_d is set to the caller’s real user ID.

       The calling process must either be in the same  PID  namespace  as  the
       process referred to by _p_i_d_f_d, or be in an ancestor of that namespace.

